01:12:00,081 --> 01:12:09,033
Now the most common mistake here is people going here, keeping the elbows bent and bringing it here, and then we don't get that anti-rotation of the core.

768
01:12:09,373 --> 01:12:22,191
So if I go here and then I really straighten out, then that cable is pulling me this way and I gotta resist that and keep my chest forward and my hands right in front of my chest, so I'm really firing up those core muscles.

769
01:12:22,171 --> 01:12:37,193
as always we want to start light you may you might start 10 or 20 pounds and as you get better we're gonna go up and up and up and we want to go basically as heavy as we can with great form I don't want to see that form breaking down we're gonna do the

770
01:12:37,173 --> 01:13:02,303
desired reps to one side and then I'm gonna flip the other way and then you're going to that right knee same exact thing so this exercise is great for not only core strength but it's functional core strength this really translates to sports you've got to be able to get to that position and then build that anti rotation because a lot of times you go to rip through and you get jammed up you get a bump from somebody and you start to fly into rotation

771
01:13:02,283 --> 01:13:08,514
But if you can maintain your posture and you can push through that contact, then you're going to be dominant on the court.

772
01:13:08,875 --> 01:13:16,508
So many people are held back because there's players that on the court, they're giving you a bump to one side of the body and we lose our posture.

773
01:13:16,528 --> 01:13:20,375
We've got to be able to keep that chest forward and get to where we want to go.

774
01:13:20,696 --> 01:13:23,160
And it all starts with strength drills like this.

