7811
11:48:16,996 --> 11:48:19,463
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

7812
11:48:19,763 --> 11:48:22,110
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

7813
11:48:22,130 --> 11:48:24,356
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

7814
11:48:24,636 --> 11:48:26,421
I'm just gonna lean forward.

7815
11:48:26,401 --> 11:48:29,128
from the hips, keeping my back nice and straight.

7816
11:48:29,449 --> 11:48:36,469
If you wanna reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe, you can, but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back.

7817
11:48:36,629 --> 11:48:41,743
So I like to just keep my hands here to make sure that I'm not rounding the upper back.

7818
11:48:42,601 --> 11:48:46,511
and I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

7819
11:48:46,531 --> 11:48:49,498
15 seconds on the other side as well.

7820
11:48:49,518 --> 11:48:51,623
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

7821
11:48:51,824 --> 11:48:53,247
So that is the back of the thigh.

7822
11:48:53,488 --> 11:48:57,758
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

7823
11:48:57,738 --> 11:49:01,488
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

7824
11:49:01,628 --> 11:49:04,055
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

7825
11:49:04,395 --> 11:49:06,721
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

7826
11:49:06,782 --> 11:49:08,626
So now look at this back foot.

7827
11:49:08,667 --> 11:49:10,110
I'm not curling the toe under.

7828
11:49:10,471 --> 11:49:14,582
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

7829
11:49:14,562 --> 11:49:17,986
and I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

7830
11:49:18,406 --> 11:49:23,752
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

7831
11:49:24,072 --> 11:49:37,347
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

7832
11:49:37,327 --> 11:49:40,591
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

7833
11:49:40,631 --> 11:49:41,973
That's the inside of the leg.

7834
11:49:42,453 --> 11:49:45,817
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side.

7835
11:49:46,418 --> 11:49:47,099
Same thing.

7836
11:49:47,439 --> 11:49:50,323
For my glute stretch, I'm going to pull this knee back.

7837
11:49:50,623 --> 11:49:53,767
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

7838
11:49:54,047 --> 11:49:57,912
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

7839
11:49:58,212 --> 11:50:02,878
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull

7840
11:50:02,858 --> 11:50:05,843
this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

7841
11:50:06,204 --> 11:50:11,693
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

7842
11:50:12,114 --> 11:50:22,352
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

7843
11:50:22,332 --> 11:50:30,208
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

7844
11:50:30,729 --> 11:50:33,514
So I'm just going to pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

7845
11:50:33,955 --> 11:50:39,987
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

7846
11:50:39,967 --> 11:50:43,031
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

7847
11:50:43,112 --> 11:50:45,275
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

7848
11:50:45,515 --> 11:50:49,160
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

7849
11:50:49,641 --> 11:50:51,223
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

7850
11:50:51,604 --> 11:50:54,128
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

7851
11:50:54,208 --> 11:50:55,149
So I'm 90 there.

7852
11:50:55,490 --> 11:50:58,274
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

7853
11:50:58,634 --> 11:51:00,918
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

7854
11:51:01,278 --> 11:51:05,985
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

7855
11:51:06,325 --> 11:51:09,450
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

7856
11:51:09,430 --> 11:51:12,354
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

7857
11:51:12,714 --> 11:51:17,481
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

7858
11:51:17,521 --> 11:51:18,963
All right.

7859
11:51:18,983 --> 11:51:24,851
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

7860
11:51:25,592 --> 11:51:29,397
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

7861
11:51:29,377 --> 11:51:34,723
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

7862
11:51:35,103 --> 11:51:38,327
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

7863
11:51:38,427 --> 11:51:41,631
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

7864
11:51:42,011 --> 11:51:51,722
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

7865
11:51:52,023 --> 11:51:58,510
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

7866
11:51:58,770 --> 11:52:04,937
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

7867
11:52:05,338 --> 11:52:09,422
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

7868
11:52:10,583 --> 11:52:14,388
A lot of us aren't gonna be able to get our heel all the way to the floor, that's okay.

7869
11:52:14,748 --> 11:52:17,952
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

7870
11:52:17,992 --> 11:52:21,996
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

7871
11:52:24,659 --> 11:52:25,300
What's up, guys?

7872
11:52:25,440 --> 11:52:29,905
Quick minimum dose advice for phase nine, day three and day six.

7873
11:52:29,925 --> 11:52:31,967
So this is our core and mobility day.

7874
11:52:32,568 --> 11:52:35,671
As you can see, don't need to cut out anything.

7875
11:52:35,892 --> 11:52:38,355
Very short, very efficient workout.

7876
11:52:38,955 --> 11:52:43,641
Since we're at six days a week, we can't have these long core and mobility workouts.

7877
11:52:44,381 --> 11:52:53,772
We would rather just get in, get it done in 20, 30 minutes, and get out of the gym because we need to recover for the next day.

7878
11:52:53,752 --> 11:52:59,244
If you have to cut anything back, just cut from four sets down to three sets, all right?

7879
11:52:59,945 --> 11:53:02,010
But don't cut out any exercises.

7880
11:53:02,130 --> 11:53:04,575
All of these exercises are absolutely essential.

7881
11:53:04,876 --> 11:53:05,517
Let's get it.

7882
11:53:14,778 --> 11:53:18,383
So for our leg raises, you're gonna go on a Roman chair if you have it.

7883
11:53:18,563 --> 11:53:21,647
If not, then you can just hang on a normal pull-up bar.

7884
11:53:21,987 --> 11:53:23,389
Either one will work just fine.

7885
11:53:23,449 --> 11:53:26,653
So if I'm in the Roman chair, I'm gonna get set up here.

7886
11:53:26,673 --> 11:53:30,038
Now, you guys are used to doing a full knee raise.

7887
11:53:30,358 --> 11:53:33,262
That bottom half is really more hip flexors.

7888
11:53:33,722 --> 11:53:35,925
The top half, from here,

7889
11:53:35,905 --> 11:53:42,054
to here to as high as I can get that knee is going to be abs, especially those lower abdominals.

7890
11:53:42,115 --> 11:53:46,441
So what I got to think about is a posterior pelvic tilt.

7891
11:53:47,262 --> 11:53:51,589
So if this is an anterior pelvic tilt, posterior is the opposite.

7892
11:53:51,689 --> 11:53:52,891
Think about like I have a tail.

7893
11:53:53,412 --> 11:53:54,994
Think about tucking that tail.

7894
11:53:55,495 --> 11:54:01,104
That motion of posterior pelvic tilt is what's going to really activate those abdominals.

7895
11:54:01,164 --> 11:54:03,347
So I'm here.

7896
11:54:03,563 --> 11:54:11,774
and I got to posteriorly tilt, think about getting my knees as high as I can, and then back down halfway.

7897
11:54:12,355 --> 11:54:18,464
So that is how we actually train the abs instead of just working the hip flexors.

7898
11:54:18,724 --> 11:54:27,276
So for our leg raises, you can either go in a Roman chair like this if you have it, if not, then we're just gonna hang from a regular pull-up bar.

7899
11:54:32,285 --> 11:54:36,730
So for my ankle toe crawls, what I'm going to do is get in a half kneeling position.

7900
11:54:37,071 --> 11:54:42,237
I'm going to work on my ankle dorsiflexion by driving my knee as far as I can over the toe.

7901
11:54:42,638 --> 11:54:45,181
And then I'm going to flow straight into my toe stretch.

7902
11:54:45,201 --> 11:54:52,670
So I got to get this knee down to the floor while keeping my big toe and my other four toes down on the ground.

7903
11:54:52,710 --> 11:54:54,192
So I'm here.

7904
11:54:54,408 --> 11:54:58,337
Now look at that position that I'm in with the toe.

7905
11:54:58,357 --> 11:55:01,405
So I'm challenging my toe mobility.

7906
11:55:01,465 --> 11:55:08,561
So what I'm going to do is just crawl in this position and I can do anywhere from four to eight reps each leg.

7907
11:55:08,581 --> 11:55:09,704
So I'm going to start at four.

7908
11:55:10,346 --> 11:55:11,809
So I'm here.

7909
11:55:12,160 --> 11:55:23,137
nice and slow, look how I crawl the hand forward, and then I ground that next foot, drive the knee over the toe, and gradually transition, all right?

7910
11:55:23,418 --> 11:55:29,207
So I'm literally just crawling, and I'm challenging my ankle mobility,

7911
11:55:29,187 --> 11:55:32,132
and my toe mobility as I go.

7912
11:55:32,352 --> 11:55:35,177
So this is essential when it comes to getting shin angles.

7913
11:55:35,197 --> 11:55:40,186
So you guys hear me talk a lot about the most explosive athletes have good shin angles.

7914
11:55:40,206 --> 11:55:47,518
If you can get horizontal shin angles, we produce more horizontal force, which means we get somewhere faster, right?

7915
11:55:47,558 --> 11:55:50,503
So horizontal force production is essential, but

7916
11:55:50,483 --> 11:56:00,874
If you get here and you lock up, right, because you don't have the ankle mobility or you have the ankle mobility but you don't have the toe mobility, we can't get that shin angle.

7917
11:56:01,214 --> 11:56:02,636
So now we have vertical shins.

7918
11:56:03,136 --> 11:56:08,442
I don't care how much force you produce with vertical shins, you can't get horizontal.

7919
11:56:08,842 --> 11:56:11,845
So half the battle is how much force we produce.

7920
11:56:11,865 --> 11:56:15,549
The other half of the battle is the angle that we're producing the force.

7921
11:56:15,909 --> 11:56:17,571
And so that's why we're training

7922
11:56:17,551 --> 11:56:24,338
This ankle mobility, you see how I got to be able to get my knee far out over my toe, which takes dorsiflexion range of motion.

7923
11:56:24,838 --> 11:56:29,743
But as I extend this hip, I need to have that toe range of motion.

7924
11:56:29,803 --> 11:56:34,287
If that toe locks up, then I don't get full extension of the hip.

7925
11:56:34,668 --> 11:56:45,398
So we need that dorsiflexion and we need that toe range of motion so that we can get these good shin angles and get full triple extension on our first step explosiveness.

7926
11:56:45,378 --> 11:56:51,709
So we're going to do anywhere from four to eight reps, and we can do anywhere from two to four sets.

7927
11:56:52,029 --> 11:56:54,854
Do this a couple days a week, up to three days a week.

7928
11:56:55,114 --> 11:56:57,959
This is a good one that you can just add to your warm-ups.

7929
11:56:58,199 --> 11:57:07,815
You could add this to your dynamic warm-ups, or if you're at home and you're bored watching TV, it's a great exercise to do just to challenge that ankle mobility and that toe mobility.

7930
11:57:08,156 --> 11:57:08,837
Let's get it.

7931
11:57:14,487 --> 11:57:18,640
So for our lateral oblique crunch, there's a lot of different ways we can do this.

7932
11:57:18,660 --> 11:57:25,763
If you have something to hold down your feet, or you have a partner to hold your feet down, then just go in a stacked position.

7933
11:57:26,418 --> 11:57:28,641
and we can just go on a BOSU ball.

7934
11:57:28,661 --> 11:57:34,370
So you'd put your hip directly on the center of that BOSU ball, and then I would come fully extended.

7935
11:57:34,730 --> 11:57:41,340
So you could either cross your arms or just keep your arms here, or you could bring one arm here and one arm across the chest.

7936
11:57:41,660 --> 11:57:43,263
I like to do that option a lot of times.

7937
11:57:43,303 --> 11:57:52,296
So in this position now, I have my whole torso hanging over the BOSU, and I'm just gonna come into a little lateral crunch, all right?

7938
11:57:52,316 --> 11:57:54,619
So it's a small range motion.

7939
11:57:54,599 --> 11:58:00,548
but I'm really getting a good workout on that top oblique, so my left oblique is fired up right now.

7940
11:58:00,928 --> 11:58:04,814
Now, I know a lot of you guys don't have a BOSU ball, so there's some other options.

7941
11:58:05,275 --> 11:58:14,548
We could go on a bench and get our torso off the bench, have a partner hold down our legs and do the same thing, or we could go on a hyperextension machine

7942
11:58:16,469 --> 11:58:20,176
and come in that way so that our feet are anchored.

7943
11:58:20,798 --> 11:58:26,088
And same thing, my body is now hanging off so I can do the same exact thing.

7944
11:58:26,649 --> 11:58:33,623
Actually, in this position, I could even hold a dumbbell if that's too easy and still get that good oblique work.

7945
11:58:34,044 --> 11:58:38,292
Now, if none of those setups work for you, then just go to a cable.

7946
11:58:41,528 --> 11:58:46,895
Bring it down to the lowest setting and we can just do a side crunch like this.

7947
11:58:47,456 --> 11:58:49,779
So any of those options work.

7948
11:58:49,799 --> 11:58:54,284
All we're looking for is a big workout, a big burn in those side obliques.

7949
11:58:54,605 --> 11:58:58,390
If the rep range is too easy for you, then go ahead and add reps.

7950
11:58:58,410 --> 11:59:02,075
We want to go pretty much to a maximal burn on this exercise.

7951
11:59:02,415 --> 11:59:03,216
Let's do it.

7952
11:59:16,375 --> 11:59:21,765
So for my single leg supported rotations, what I'm going to do is step in front of a bench.

7953
11:59:22,005 --> 11:59:28,657
If you don't have a bench, you can actually put your back foot on a wall and get the sole flat to the wall just for a little added stability.

7954
11:59:29,077 --> 11:59:32,864
Now if your stability is very good, you could just do this on one leg.

7955
11:59:33,125 --> 11:59:39,075
But for most of you guys, I want you doing it assisted and we can actually get a little more mobility if it is assisted.

7956
11:59:39,055 --> 11:59:40,416
So take a big step forward.

7957
11:59:40,677 --> 11:59:42,078
I'm going to throw my back foot up.

7958
11:59:42,158 --> 11:59:47,503
Instead of pointing the toe like I normally would for a rear foot elevated, I'm curling that toe.

7959
11:59:48,183 --> 11:59:54,609
If you have a band, pull that band tight and get it up above the head or any type of light dowel would work.

7960
11:59:55,310 --> 12:00:05,940
Slight bend in that front knee and now I hinge from the hips and from here I'm just rotating as far as I can and then back to neutral.

7961
12:00:05,920 --> 12:00:35,860
and as far as i can the other way and back to neutral okay i'm going to feel that stretch all through the hips lateral portion of the hips medial portion of the hips my upper back and my shoulder my thoracic spine i'm getting a lot of really good mobility so we should feel this a little bit throughout the entire body we're really mobilizing the entire body and getting some stability at the same time let's do it

7962
12:00:46,403 --> 12:00:57,119
So for my rolling cross touch, what I'm going to do is I'm just going to bring my feet off the ground and I'm going to bring my hands off the ground so I'm in this hollow body position.

7963
12:00:57,360 --> 12:01:06,894
I don't want to have an excessive curve in the lower back, so I want to think about tilting my pelvis, pretend like you have a tail, and think about tucking that tail so your back is nice and flat to the floor.

7964
12:01:07,315 --> 12:01:09,238
And then from there, I'm just rolling.

7965
12:01:09,218 --> 12:01:11,100
So my feet still aren't going to touch the ground.

7966
12:01:11,140 --> 12:01:12,822
My hands are still not going to touch the ground.

7967
12:01:13,162 --> 12:01:13,983
I'm going to roll over.

7968
12:01:14,023 --> 12:01:15,405
I'm going to roll over again.

7969
12:01:15,525 --> 12:01:19,890
So I end up in the same position and then I'm going to go cross body.

7970
12:01:19,910 --> 12:01:25,096
So when I roll that way, I'm going to go left hand touches the right toe and back down.

7971
12:01:25,336 --> 12:01:28,400
And again, left hand, right toe back down.

7972
12:01:28,820 --> 12:01:30,602
And then I roll back to the other side.

7973
12:01:31,022 --> 12:01:33,085
So now I'll end up in the same position.

7974
12:01:33,465 --> 12:01:36,829
Now, once I'm back here, I go right hand left.

7975
12:01:36,809 --> 12:01:39,154
So right hand, left toe.

7976
12:01:39,435 --> 12:01:41,880
And then again, I roll back to the other side.

7977
12:01:42,341 --> 12:01:50,979
So the reason that we are rolling is it is one of the most fundamental movement patterns that humans have and we lose it over time.

7978
12:01:51,180 --> 12:01:56,050
But when we roll, we're training so many core muscles and we're training them reactively.

7979
12:01:56,030 --> 12:02:01,541
It is a training stimulus for the core that we can't get with any traditional core exercise.

7980
12:02:01,821 --> 12:02:10,478
So it's really building up that core stability and it's building it up naturally and reflexively which is really going to carry over to all of our athletic movements.

7981
12:02:25,038 --> 12:02:28,465
For the hip flexor tilt, I'm just gonna use a box of bench or a chair.

7982
12:02:29,186 --> 12:02:34,015
I'm gonna get my back foot up so the top of my foot is on the bench.

7983
12:02:34,055 --> 12:02:35,638
I'm not gonna go with my toe on the bench.

7984
12:02:36,039 --> 12:02:38,584
Top of the foot on the bench, I'm just gonna come down.

7985
12:02:38,905 --> 12:02:43,333
Now I wanna get my heel touching my hips or as close as I can get to that.

7986
12:02:43,313 --> 12:02:45,316
If you have a pad, that'd be better.

7987
12:02:45,356 --> 12:02:46,658
I wouldn't do this on hardwood.

7988
12:02:46,838 --> 12:02:48,521
Make sure you got a nice, soft surface.

7989
12:02:49,022 --> 12:02:49,783
So I'm touching.

7990
12:02:50,183 --> 12:02:54,049
I'm in a straight line from my knee all the way up through my shoulders.

7991
12:02:54,590 --> 12:02:55,752
And I'm just going to sit here.

7992
12:02:55,892 --> 12:02:57,454
First, I'm going to get in a good position.

7993
12:02:57,955 --> 12:02:59,918
This ankle should be under that knee.

7994
12:03:00,559 --> 12:03:04,365
I should already feel a little bit of a stretch in that hip flexor, a little bit in the quad as well.

7995
12:03:04,786 --> 12:03:07,289
Now from here, I'm getting nice and wide.

7996
12:03:07,309 --> 12:03:08,471
I'm not going to lean forward.

7997
12:03:08,812 --> 12:03:10,975
And I'm going arms out.

7998
12:03:10,955 --> 12:03:22,686
and together now at the top with straight elbows not bent elbows at the top I'm going to lean towards the lead leg so that's to my right as far as I can

7999
12:03:23,408 --> 12:03:27,076
hold for five, and then come right back, okay?

8000
12:03:27,096 --> 12:03:36,156
And then we're hands down, all the way up, hands together, nice and tall, elongate through the spine, and lean towards that lead leg.

8001
12:03:36,196 --> 12:03:42,430
Three, two, one, back down, and we're back through.

8002
12:03:45,548 --> 12:03:46,229
What's up, guys?

8003
12:03:46,349 --> 12:03:51,137
Quick minimum dose advice for phase 10, day one and day four.

8004
12:03:51,397 --> 12:03:53,100
So this is our lower body strength day.

8005
12:03:53,501 --> 12:03:56,706
As you can see, already very short and efficient.

8006
12:03:56,746 --> 12:03:59,150
We probably shouldn't have to cut anything out here.

8007
12:03:59,531 --> 12:04:03,577
Now, just because it is short and efficient doesn't mean that it's easy.

8008
12:04:03,898 --> 12:04:05,440
It is very high intensity.

8009
12:04:05,500 --> 12:04:08,485
So these rear foot elevated split squats.

8010
12:04:08,465 --> 12:04:11,189
Alright, six sets of two.

8011
12:04:11,669 --> 12:04:16,977
So you might see two and think that it's easy, but two means that, of course, we're max intensity.

8012
12:04:17,497 --> 12:04:22,785
So we're gonna be loaded up, very heavy, and six sets is quite a lot.

8013
12:04:22,825 --> 12:04:25,288
Make sure you're taking plenty of rest time in between.

8014
12:04:25,348 --> 12:04:28,212
We want full recovery.

8015
12:04:28,192 --> 12:04:46,977
Now if you have any type of knee problems, patellar tendonitis, anything like that, that might be too intense, so you could go back to another hip dominant exercise, a deadlift or something else, but we still want to get that high intensity just in a pain-free zone, so find an exercise that you're pain-free.

8016
12:04:46,957 --> 12:04:50,285
Full range of motion RDL, so four sets of five.

8017
12:04:50,687 --> 12:04:52,752
This is not gonna be maximally loaded.

8018
12:04:52,832 --> 12:04:55,800
We're more so going for full range of motion.

8019
12:04:56,441 --> 12:05:00,231
So for some of you guys, that's gonna be a normal RDL, just full range.

8020
12:05:00,492 --> 12:05:03,058
For some of you, we're actually gonna elevate on a box

8021
12:05:03,038 --> 12:05:09,388
or a bumper plate, and we're getting that max range, I want you guys to start to strengthen in that end range.

8022
12:05:09,629 --> 12:05:17,441
That's where most injuries happen, is at our near end range of that muscle length, so that's why we're going full range of motion there.

8023
12:05:18,823 --> 12:05:22,189
The mini band ankle inversion, eversion, definitely don't skip that.

8024
12:05:22,529 --> 12:05:29,721
One leg functional calf raise, if you have to skip one, this is the one to take out, but definitely try to leave it in, it's a very good exercise.

8025
12:05:29,853 --> 12:05:31,376
We're going to get through a foam roller circuit.

8026
12:05:31,516 --> 12:05:33,219
I have a long roller from Skills.

8027
12:05:33,479 --> 12:05:40,350
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

8028
12:05:40,711 --> 12:05:46,140
So the first muscle that we're going to focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

8029
12:05:46,120 --> 12:05:49,223
So you can either go this way or you could go vertical.

8030
12:05:49,564 --> 12:05:58,714
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

8031
12:05:58,734 --> 12:06:05,141
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

8032
12:06:05,862 --> 12:06:12,229
Or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

8033
12:06:12,209 --> 12:06:14,552
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

8034
12:06:14,572 --> 12:06:15,974
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

8035
12:06:16,314 --> 12:06:24,705
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

8036
12:06:25,085 --> 12:06:36,900
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

8037
12:06:37,100 --> 12:06:38,882
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

8038
12:06:39,182 --> 12:06:42,585
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

8039
12:06:42,665 --> 12:06:50,892
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

8040
12:06:50,952 --> 12:06:54,716
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles.

8041
12:06:55,116 --> 12:06:56,477
And our next one we're flipping over.

8042
12:06:56,538 --> 12:06:59,680
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

8043
12:06:59,740 --> 12:07:07,107
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll.

8044
12:07:07,087 --> 12:07:29,753
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh so i'm getting my whole quad in this one and if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee then start here and finish and then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee

8045
12:07:29,733 --> 12:07:31,497
Our next one is going to be for the adductors.

8046
12:07:31,517 --> 12:07:32,639
That's the inside of the leg.

8047
12:07:32,820 --> 12:07:34,563
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

8048
12:07:34,884 --> 12:07:48,653
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside of the top of the knee all the way up through the groin area.

8049
12:07:50,422 --> 12:07:57,232
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

8050
12:07:57,432 --> 12:08:00,998
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

8051
12:08:01,298 --> 12:08:04,563
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

8052
12:08:05,104 --> 12:08:07,808
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

8053
12:08:07,848 --> 12:08:09,490
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

8054
12:08:09,811 --> 12:08:13,637
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

8055
12:08:14,077 --> 12:08:17,222
And I'm gonna really loosen up that fascia.

8056
12:08:17,202 --> 12:08:25,049
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day, we're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

8057
12:08:25,390 --> 12:08:28,092
We got to really start to loosen that up.

8058
12:08:29,694 --> 12:08:34,258
Now after that, it's optional if you want to go up and work on the smaller muscles of the hip.

8059
12:08:34,498 --> 12:08:38,001
You can work on the smaller muscles of the shoulder up against a wall if you'd like to.

8060
12:08:38,762 --> 12:08:43,206
I just want to make sure that we get that foam roll circuit and we get that lacrosse ball on the feet.

8061
12:08:43,586 --> 12:08:45,268
Everything else is extra credit.

8062
12:08:48,337 --> 12:08:52,083
So the first thing we're gonna do for our dynamic warmup is stationary skips.

8063
12:08:52,363 --> 12:08:54,947
Now for all of these, you can take them on the move if you want.

8064
12:08:55,007 --> 12:08:58,973
For right now, I'm just gonna do them stationary just in case you don't have much room.

8065
12:08:59,474 --> 12:09:06,244
So we're just going up with the right, up with the left, just like we're running, and vice versa on that other side.

8066
12:09:06,224 --> 12:09:10,069
All right, I'm staying stiff with the feet, so I'm not letting my heel collapse.

8067
12:09:10,109 --> 12:09:11,491
I want to stay on the ball of the foot.

8068
12:09:11,911 --> 12:09:15,035
And I'm just going to go about 50% of my max speed.

8069
12:09:15,376 --> 12:09:16,837
I'm not going too fast yet.

8070
12:09:17,118 --> 12:09:19,321
We're going to go for 30 seconds on the skips.

8071
12:09:19,541 --> 12:09:24,087
For the seal jacks, it's basically like a jumping jack, except I'm going to coordinate arms with the legs.

8072
12:09:24,107 --> 12:09:28,272
So I'm going to go over with the right and over with the right hand at the same time.

8073
12:09:28,693 --> 12:09:32,217
Back to neutral, over with the left, over with the left hand.

8074
12:09:32,557 --> 12:09:34,560
And then I'm just going to speed that up.

8075
12:09:34,540 --> 12:09:36,803
I'm gonna get a good little rhythm going here.

8076
12:09:37,424 --> 12:09:42,450
Maybe somewhere between 30 and 50% of your max speed, so we're not all out.

8077
12:09:42,911 --> 12:09:44,492
We're just getting that blood flowing.

8078
12:09:44,773 --> 12:09:46,135
We're gonna go for 30 seconds.

8079
12:09:46,295 --> 12:09:50,640
For the over-under hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna go over with the left leg.

8080
12:09:50,981 --> 12:09:52,803
I'm gonna come down, touch the toe.

8081
12:09:52,843 --> 12:09:57,589
Now, if you can't touch the toe without rounding the back, then just go mid-shin or even to the knees.

8082
12:09:57,849 --> 12:10:02,455
Just as low as we can, but we don't want to go to the point where we have to round our back.

8083
12:10:02,435 --> 12:10:10,150
Okay, so I'm gonna get down, touch my toes, back to neutral, and then I cross over the other way, and I'm going the other side.

8084
12:10:13,792 --> 12:10:18,158
All right, we're going to go five over and back, so it's 10 total touches.

8085
12:10:18,379 --> 12:10:23,707
For the quad hamstring leans, I'm just going to go into a quad stretch, so I'm going to pull my right foot up.

8086
12:10:23,987 --> 12:10:29,215
I want to make sure that my heel is touching my hip in that position, and I'm not out like this.

8087
12:10:29,295 --> 12:10:30,537
I'm keeping that knee in.

8088
12:10:30,957 --> 12:10:38,989
Now from there, I'm going to keep the left knee straight, and I'm just going to go into a lean as low as I can.

8089
12:10:38,969 --> 12:11:00,825
so i'm getting a really good stretch in the quad by going down and getting my knee back i'm getting it into the hip flexor a little bit more and i'm getting this hamstring a good stretch and i'm also working on my balance at the same time i'm going to go five leans and then i'm going to switch over five leans on the other side for the hip opener we're going to go push up position

8090
12:11:01,717 --> 12:11:05,364
I'm going to go left leg up outside this left hand.

8091
12:11:05,785 --> 12:11:08,169
Now from here, I'm just going to rotate up and hold.

8092
12:11:08,209 --> 12:11:10,233
One, two, three.

8093
12:11:10,293 --> 12:11:12,577
Now I'm going to go elbow down towards the floor.

8094
12:11:12,898 --> 12:11:14,922
We might not be able to touch the elbow to the ground.

8095
12:11:14,962 --> 12:11:15,543
That's okay.

8096
12:11:15,883 --> 12:11:17,466
Let's just get as low as we can there.

8097
12:11:17,947 --> 12:11:18,949
One, two, three.

8098
12:11:18,929 --> 12:11:20,633
three, and back up.

8099
12:11:20,693 --> 12:11:22,697
So three there, three there.

8100
12:11:23,018 --> 12:11:29,311
I'm gonna go five cycles, and then I'm gonna switch to the other side, five and five, same thing.

8101
12:11:29,411 --> 12:11:32,879
For the adductor hip flexor combo, I'm going lateral with one skip.

8102
12:11:32,919 --> 12:11:36,607
So it's one skip lateral, and I'm going down, I'm trying to touch this floor.

8103
12:11:37,068 --> 12:11:39,553
Keep this knee straight because I wanna get that

8104
12:11:39,533 --> 12:11:59,787
stretch on the inside of the leg in this adductor i'm going back with the hip like i would in a normal squat and i'm getting nice and low touch the floor and i'm popping out to that other side all right so let's just hold about one second at the bottom and pop out i need five of these down and back so it's 10 total and then i immediately go into a hip flexor

8105
12:11:59,767 --> 12:12:14,434
Same thing with the skip and now I land I turn this foot forward I pivot I'm here knee a couple inches off the floor I'm gonna rotate hold one and then come back and it's to the other side

8106
12:12:19,358 --> 12:12:25,885
At the bottom of this stretch, I'm gonna get that really good stretch in the hip flexor, and I always need to turn towards the lead leg.

8107
12:12:25,905 --> 12:12:27,366
Don't make the mistake of going here.

8108
12:12:27,747 --> 12:12:35,795
You need to rotate towards that lead leg as far as we can, and I'm gonna feel it through that hip flexor, and then I pop out to the other side.

8109
12:12:35,915 --> 12:12:41,781
For the overhead deep squat progression, I'm going high with the hands, basic squat stance so I'm a little wider than shoulder width.

8110
12:12:41,861 --> 12:12:45,585
I can point the toes out just a little bit, and then I'm just gonna go here,

8111
12:12:45,818 --> 12:13:04,249
it's going to be a hamstring stretch one two three keep the hands down and come into a deep squat try to stay back on your heels if you can one two three rock forward so i'm on my toes now drive the knees out with the elbows hold one

8112
12:13:04,229 --> 12:13:23,453
two, three, rock it back, heels back down to the ground, one, two, three, I need one hand up, two hand up, not out here, we need it back behind the head, and stand, alright, so that's one cycle, we're going to go for three of these, so it's one, two, three,

8113
12:13:23,787 --> 12:13:52,849
one two three lean one two three heels back down one two three one up two up stand all right if you cannot get down in that deep position then let's just go as low as we can right here and then on the lean it's just gonna look like this all right and then we're gonna come back hopefully parallel to the floor one up two up stand we just got to get as low as we can and every day try to progress so that we can get lower and lower better range of motion so

8114
12:13:52,829 --> 12:13:55,672
So for the hip bridge, it's gonna be a very basic hip bridge.

8115
12:13:55,732 --> 12:13:58,054
Even if you're an advanced athlete, I want you to do this version.

8116
12:13:58,434 --> 12:14:01,236
So we're gonna get up nice and flat in a hip bridge.

8117
12:14:01,557 --> 12:14:03,258
I need to be flat through the hips.

8118
12:14:03,538 --> 12:14:05,941
I'm driving through my midfoot right now.

8119
12:14:06,481 --> 12:14:10,905
And now I'm just gonna squeeze my glutes together as hard as I possibly can.

8120
12:14:11,285 --> 12:14:14,068
So just holding in this position isn't that difficult.

8121
12:14:14,348 --> 12:14:22,195
But if I squeeze my glutes together as hard as I can so they're rock solid, I'm gonna get a really good isometric contraction.

8122
12:14:22,175 --> 12:14:24,520
And I'm going to activate a lot of muscle fibers.

8123
12:14:25,582 --> 12:14:32,537
And we're just going to hold in this position for 25 seconds with a full squeeze and then relax.

8124
12:14:32,557 --> 12:14:36,705
This is all for hip activation, getting those glutes fired up.

8125
12:14:36,725 --> 12:14:38,469
It's the most powerful muscle in the body.

8126
12:14:38,709 --> 12:14:42,377
So we got to get them revved up for our session.

8127
12:14:49,748 --> 12:14:55,534
So for our rear foot elevated split squat, if you have a Smith machine, I advise that we use that.

8128
12:14:55,734 --> 12:15:03,261
It's one of the best things that we can do for the RFPSS simply because we have enough stability to where we can really get max intent.

8129
12:15:03,301 --> 12:15:07,906
We can load up the weight and get explosive without worrying about the balance factor.

8130
12:15:08,206 --> 12:15:12,711
So it's one of the best ways to build pure strength and power in the rear foot elevated.

8131
12:15:12,731 --> 12:15:17,055
If you don't have a Smith machine, I would rather not go barbell.

8132
12:15:17,035 --> 12:15:18,944
I would rather just go to dumbbells.

8133
12:15:18,964 --> 12:15:22,018
So we're going to do the same thing, just with dumbbells as heavy as you can.

8134
12:15:22,279 --> 12:15:24,750
So for this rear foot elevated,

8135
12:15:25,675 --> 12:15:28,498
We are going to go balanced glute and quad.

8136
12:15:28,538 --> 12:15:31,722
So sometimes we go ankle underneath the knee.

8137
12:15:31,762 --> 12:15:32,703
That's going to be more glute.

8138
12:15:32,983 --> 12:15:42,714
Right now at that bottom position, when I got my rear foot up and I'm down on my knee, I want my knee directly over the toe.

8139
12:15:43,074 --> 12:15:44,276
So not beyond the toe.

8140
12:15:44,396 --> 12:15:46,638
I just want it right over that toe.

8141
12:15:46,999 --> 12:15:49,101
So that's the position that we're going to work with.

8142
12:15:50,667 --> 12:15:56,715
Of course, we would always start with just the bar, and then we would gradually load up heavier and heavier if we're feeling good.

8143
12:15:57,737 --> 12:16:03,585
So unrack it, and then it's control down, tap that knee, explode up as hard as I can.

8144
12:16:03,625 --> 12:16:08,351
Now if I'm loaded heavy, of course I'm not gonna be able to get up very fast.

8145
12:16:08,631 --> 12:16:20,067
It should be taking between half a second and a second to get from the bottom all the way to the top, but we have the intent to move that bar with a fast speed.

8146
12:16:20,047 --> 12:16:24,112
Okay, so we have two options for unracking that bar.

8147
12:16:24,212 --> 12:16:31,140
So the first option is what I just described where we get that position from there and then we go up and we unrack.

8148
12:16:31,481 --> 12:16:44,957
The other option is just to mark out maybe with a piece of tape or a weight where our toe will be and then I can lift up with two and then I can get that toe right where that piece of tape is and then I can go back from there.

8149
12:16:44,937 --> 12:16:49,924
With the Smith machine, we have the luxury of doing that since we have some stability, we don't have to worry about the balance as much.

8150
12:16:50,325 --> 12:16:54,571
So I can go here, and then I can work from there.

8151
12:16:55,252 --> 12:17:00,179
Now, if you have the dumbbells, you don't have that luxury, so I would rather see you start from the bottom up.

8152
12:17:00,740 --> 12:17:07,930
I would rather see the dumbbells be on the floor, and then we set up in our position, grab the dumbbells, and then we go from there.

8153
12:17:08,291 --> 12:17:09,713
All right, let's get after it.

8154
12:17:25,201 --> 12:17:32,618
So for our full range of motion RDLs, what I'm going to do is I can either use a barbell or if you have a trap bar, that's even better.

8155
12:17:33,199 --> 12:17:35,123
So I'm going to show you guys with the barbell.

8156
12:17:35,143 --> 12:17:39,393
So I would go with my alternating grip just outside hip width.

8157
12:17:39,613 --> 12:17:43,041
So for my first rep, I want to do a normal deadlift up.

8158
12:17:43,628 --> 12:17:47,555
Now from here, for an RDL, we want a slight bend in the knee.

8159
12:17:47,875 --> 12:17:50,379
From there, I am not bending my knees anymore.

8160
12:17:50,439 --> 12:17:51,561
From there, it's just a hip hinge.

8161
12:17:51,902 --> 12:17:56,770
So I'm essentially just pushing my hips backwards as I go into this RDL.

8162
12:17:56,790 --> 12:18:01,778
So I'm bringing the bar close down my body, all right?

8163
12:18:02,199 --> 12:18:07,628
Full range all the way, and back up, okay?

8164
12:18:08,435 --> 12:18:15,044
Notice that I was able to get to the floor, so I'm probably not at my maximal range of motion.

8165
12:18:15,705 --> 12:18:22,995
For a lot of you guys, the majority of you guys, you are full range and you won't quite be able to touch the floor, so that's fine.

8166
12:18:23,516 --> 12:18:26,821
But for somebody who has that range of motion, we actually wanna go elevated.

8167
12:18:27,241 --> 12:18:30,927
So first progression, I would just stand on one bumper plate.

8168
12:18:30,947 --> 12:18:37,716
For me, I've been doing these a while, so I'm gonna actually stand on two bumper plates, make sure we're nice and stable there.

8169
12:18:39,198 --> 12:18:41,342
And then, as always, I'm going to deadlift it up.

8170
12:18:43,487 --> 12:18:45,150
Same thing, same form.

8171
12:18:47,114 --> 12:18:47,676
RDL.

8172
12:18:48,056 --> 12:18:53,728
Now notice I can get a lot lower to the point where I can't quite reach the floor.

8173
12:18:54,309 --> 12:18:55,692
Alright, so it's the same form.

8174
12:18:56,334 --> 12:18:58,438
I'm going to get a huge stretch.

8175
12:18:58,823 --> 12:19:02,270
in the hamstrings at that bottom position, all right?

8176
12:19:02,430 --> 12:19:11,388
Now, common mistakes is people start getting competitive and start pushing for more range of motion, and to do that, they start rounding their back.

8177
12:19:11,488 --> 12:19:13,111
We do not wanna round the back.

8178
12:19:13,412 --> 12:19:18,722
I wanna keep spinal integrity, so I want to keep that neutral spine

8179
12:19:18,702 --> 12:19:22,690
where I can keep my back completely straight all the way through.

8180
12:19:22,750 --> 12:19:26,097
We're getting all of that range of motion just through the hips.

8181
12:19:26,779 --> 12:19:31,328
Now because of the added range of motion, you're going to want to start very light.

8182
12:19:31,408 --> 12:19:37,521
So start with the barbell, maybe go up to 25s on each side and then 35s on each side.

8183
12:19:37,501 --> 12:19:41,668
Most of you guys aren't going to need much more than 45 pounds on each side.

8184
12:19:41,768 --> 12:19:43,111
Some of you guys are super strong.

8185
12:19:43,491 --> 12:19:44,994
You can progress past that point.

8186
12:19:45,515 --> 12:19:47,519
Majority of you guys are going to be fine with this.

8187
12:19:47,839 --> 12:19:55,653
Keep in mind, this is not an exercise that creates a huge burn, but the next day you're going to be very sore.

8188
12:19:56,795 --> 12:20:01,483
This exercise is known for causing delayed onset muscle soreness.

8189
12:20:01,463 --> 12:20:11,115
Two days after you're probably still going to be pretty sore from this because that eccentric is so long and slow and the range of motion is so big.

8190
12:20:11,376 --> 12:20:15,281
We're getting a lot of those microscopic tears in the muscle fibers.

8191
12:20:15,301 --> 12:20:18,284
So great exercise but it's going to cause a lot of soreness.

8192
12:20:18,785 --> 12:20:22,370
So do not load up too heavy your first session.

8193
12:20:22,390 --> 12:20:24,332
Let's make gradual improvements.

8194
12:20:35,146 --> 12:20:46,487
So for our miniband inversion, eversion, what I'm gonna do is get this miniband sort of around the ball of the foot or around midfoot, and I'm just gonna space it out so I have a little bit of tension there.

8195
12:20:46,828 --> 12:20:53,661
Now if your miniband is super light and you don't have a stronger one, then you're gonna go even further out to get more tension on it.

8196
12:20:54,687 --> 12:20:56,348
So for me, I got pretty good tension.

8197
12:20:56,689 --> 12:21:00,572
So I'm going maybe a little more narrow than shoulders width apart.

8198
12:21:00,993 --> 12:21:04,556
Now from here, most of you guys are used to the windshield wipers.

8199
12:21:04,996 --> 12:21:07,218
So that's abduction adduction.

8200
12:21:07,438 --> 12:21:12,583
But what we want to do is actually turn the soles of the feet in and then the soles of the feet out.

8201
12:21:12,963 --> 12:21:15,065
It's going to be a small range of motion.

8202
12:21:15,386 --> 12:21:17,007
But what I mean is this.

8203
12:21:17,047 --> 12:21:24,694
So I'm dropping the soles of the feet in and then I'm pushing the soles of the feet out as far as I can.

8204
12:21:24,674 --> 12:21:49,129
out and with that inwards movement naturally you're gonna get some adduction so the toes are gonna point towards each other so it is sort of like your typical windshield wiper but we want to think about this movement instead of this movement so what we're doing with this exercise we're really strengthening up the ankles we're strengthening up the sides of the shins now think about how you sprain an ankle with an inversion sprain

8205
12:21:49,109 --> 12:21:51,231
that foot is coming to the inside.

8206
12:21:51,551 --> 12:21:57,256
So that's what we want to get here is we want to get there and then we're pushing against resistance to get it back.

8207
12:21:57,576 --> 12:22:08,566
So we're strengthening up all of those, the muscles on that lateral aspect of the ankle, the foot, the shin, so that we can really help reduce the risk of sprained ankles.

8208
12:22:08,866 --> 12:22:15,372
But also it's good because when we go to jump, we need good co-contraction around the ankle so that we're nice and stable.

8209
12:22:15,672 --> 12:22:17,934
This is a major key for one foot jumping.

8210
12:22:18,314 --> 12:22:19,015
Let's do it.

8211
12:22:31,246 --> 12:22:35,712
So for the one leg functional calf raise, you can either do this barefoot or you could use shoes.

8212
12:22:36,073 --> 12:22:39,137
Ideally, if you're in a good situation, go barefoot.

8213
12:22:39,197 --> 12:22:42,241
So find either a stair, a step, or a weight.

8214
12:22:42,602 --> 12:22:45,326
Make sure that weight is heavy so that it doesn't fly up at you.

8215
12:22:45,967 --> 12:22:47,749
And you're gonna go ball of the foot on.

8216
12:22:47,789 --> 12:22:51,955
So about half of your foot is gonna be on that edge

8217
12:22:52,812 --> 12:22:54,634
You're going to hold on to something for support.

8218
12:22:54,955 --> 12:22:57,298
I don't want you trying this without support just yet.

8219
12:22:58,079 --> 12:22:59,741
We're going to stand on one leg.

8220
12:22:59,762 --> 12:23:04,748
Instead of a normal calf raise where it's just the ankle, we're going to get the knee and the hip involved.

8221
12:23:05,049 --> 12:23:06,691
So it's almost like a quarter squat.

8222
12:23:06,751 --> 12:23:11,057
So like a partial squat, and then you finish with the calf raise.

8223
12:23:11,498 --> 12:23:12,359
Okay, so it's here.

8224
12:23:13,100 --> 12:23:14,502
Finish high with the calf raise.

8225
12:23:14,522 --> 12:23:16,424
You've got to explode up on your toes.

8226
12:23:16,865 --> 12:23:21,351
So it's squat, press, squat, press, squat, press.

8227
12:23:21,331 --> 12:23:26,100
Notice this other leg is driving, just like in a single leg vertical jump.

8228
12:23:26,120 --> 12:23:29,346
So we're back with that left leg, drive with the left leg.

8229
12:23:29,587 --> 12:23:31,891
Back with the left leg, drive with the left leg.

8230
12:23:32,633 --> 12:23:35,378
Notice I'm focusing on stiffness here with this foot.

8231
12:23:35,638 --> 12:23:38,484
So as I go down, I'm not letting it collapse.

8232
12:23:38,504 --> 12:23:41,770
I'm keeping it completely even with that ledge.

8233
12:23:42,745 --> 12:24:11,250
keep it even keep it stiff and then at the top drive up as high as we can okay so sometimes it is good to isolate with normal calf raises but this is a much more functional way of doing it and this teaches our glutes and our quads and our hamstrings to send energy down through the calves and then down through the feet to reach the floor to actually produce power that we need in the vertical jump so normally this exercise should just be body weight especially for phase one but some of our pros uh

8234
12:24:11,230 --> 12:24:14,058
More of our advanced athletes, we will go weighted vest.

8235
12:24:14,078 --> 12:24:18,451
You could go 10, 15, 20 pound weighted vest, but still hold on for support.

8236
12:24:18,691 --> 12:24:22,843
Not necessary, but if you are advanced, you can use that weighted vest up front.

8237
12:24:47,208 --> 12:24:51,232
For our 7-11 breathing, what we're going to do is just lay down on our back.

8238
12:24:51,373 --> 12:24:53,154
Make sure that you're in a relaxed place.

8239
12:24:53,215 --> 12:24:55,917
I know you're probably in a gym, so it might be a little bit loud.

8240
12:24:55,958 --> 12:25:02,465
If you have headphones, throw on some headphones, throw on some slow music, maybe some yoga or meditation type music.

8241
12:25:02,985 --> 12:25:09,192
And all I'm going to do is just lay down and I'm going to breathe in through my nose for seven seconds.

8242
12:25:09,212 --> 12:25:12,996
And I'm also going to breathe out through my nose for 11 seconds.

8243
12:25:12,976 --> 12:25:16,843
So I'm not going to open my mouth for this whole drill.

8244
12:25:17,284 --> 12:25:21,653
So it's in through the nose and I want to make sure that my stomach is rising.

8245
12:25:21,693 --> 12:25:29,027
Your chest will naturally rise a little bit, but most of you when you think of deep breathing, what happens is your belly stays still.

8246
12:25:30,037 --> 12:25:32,741
and your chest puffs up and your shoulders elevate.

8247
12:25:33,121 --> 12:25:39,850
That means we are not actually getting all of that air, all of that oxygen down to the deepest part of the lungs.

8248
12:25:40,391 --> 12:25:46,178
So I want you to think about letting the belly rise as you take that breath in, okay?

8249
12:25:46,198 --> 12:25:49,863
So we're here, in deep through the nose, let the stomach rise.

8250
12:25:52,146 --> 12:25:56,432
Seven seconds, once you finish that seven seconds, it's out through the nose.

8251
12:25:59,365 --> 12:26:01,288
and that stomach will fall, okay?

8252
12:26:02,009 --> 12:26:04,653
Initially, this is very uncomfortable for guys.

8253
12:26:05,414 --> 12:26:14,447
I've had NBA players that absolutely hated this until they're in their third or fourth session and they started to get used to it because you do have to learn how to pace your breaths.

8254
12:26:15,228 --> 12:26:23,220
If you just breathe in as hard as you can and breathe out as hard as you can, you're not gonna be able to go for seven in and 11 out.

8255
12:26:23,240 --> 12:26:27,246
You gotta learn to go very slow and pace that breath, okay?

8256
12:26:27,226 --> 12:26:56,187
this is doing a lot of stuff I mean for one we're helping you expand the lungs we're teaching you how to to breathe yourself into a more calmer state but from a recovery standpoint this could possibly be the best thing that you can do after a workout better than foam rolling better than static stretching because after a workout we're in that sympathetic state that's that fight that's that workout that's that game mode okay I got to get you to the parasympathetic state that's relax recover all recovery

8257
12:26:56,403 --> 12:26:58,908
happens in the parasympathetic state.

8258
12:26:59,449 --> 12:27:03,557
Okay, so if I'm still hyped up after the workout, I'm killing my recovery.

8259
12:27:03,938 --> 12:27:17,584
So as soon as we're done with the workout, we wanna do these breathing exercises to get us into that parasympathetic state and then I can really start to aid my recovery and I'm back better than ever tomorrow, ready to train again.

8260
12:27:19,336 --> 12:27:22,641
So the first one for our static stretch is just going to be a basic quad stretch.

8261
12:27:22,681 --> 12:27:26,947
So I'm just going to go heel to hip and I'm just going to get up nice and tall.

8262
12:27:27,227 --> 12:27:34,037
I'm going to raise my opposite hand and I'm just going to hold for 15 seconds and then I'm going to switch over to the other side.

8263
12:27:34,337 --> 12:27:37,822
If you need to hold on to something for balance, that's okay.

8264
12:27:38,102 --> 12:27:45,613
We're not going to get the same arm raise, but you can hold on and just make sure that we're nice and tall and that heel is touching the hip the whole time.

8265
12:27:45,593 --> 12:27:49,944
For the elevated hamstring stretch, I'm just gonna use a bench, a box, a chair.

8266
12:27:50,365 --> 12:27:52,330
I'm just gonna get my heel on there.

8267
12:27:52,670 --> 12:27:54,876
That toe is pointed up towards the ceiling.

8268
12:27:55,157 --> 12:27:57,222
And then I'm just gonna hinge from the hips.

8269
12:27:57,502 --> 12:27:59,728
So I don't wanna lean forward and round my back.

8270
12:28:00,029 --> 12:28:01,813
I'm just gonna lean forward.

8271
12:28:01,793 --> 12:28:04,498
from the hips, keeping my back nice and straight.

8272
12:28:04,838 --> 12:28:11,831
If you wanna reach down and touch your ankle or touch your toe, you can, but don't reach down and start to bend over from the back.

8273
12:28:12,011 --> 12:28:16,840
So I like to just keep my hands here to make sure that I'm not rounding the upper back.

8274
12:28:18,142 --> 12:28:22,570
And I'm just gonna hold 15 seconds, and then I'm gonna switch over to the other side.

8275
12:28:22,887 --> 12:28:24,749
15 seconds on the other side as well.

8276
12:28:24,869 --> 12:28:27,012
We should feel this stretch all in the hamstrings.

8277
12:28:27,192 --> 12:28:28,634
So that is the back of the thigh.

8278
12:28:28,854 --> 12:28:33,059
We'll feel it a little bit down into the calves, but we should feel it mostly hamstrings.

8279
12:28:33,179 --> 12:28:36,863
For our adductor stretch, I'm just gonna go one leg out.

8280
12:28:37,023 --> 12:28:39,446
I need to lock out this knee so I'm nice and straight.

8281
12:28:39,786 --> 12:28:42,109
I'm gonna sit back onto my heel.

8282
12:28:42,469 --> 12:28:44,031
So now look at this back foot.

8283
12:28:44,051 --> 12:28:45,473
I'm not curling the toe under.

8284
12:28:45,853 --> 12:28:49,958
I'm putting it down like this so the top of my foot is flat to the floor.

8285
12:28:49,938 --> 12:28:53,363
and I'm going to sit my hips back to that heel.

8286
12:28:53,783 --> 12:28:59,151
Now from there, I can already feel a little bit of a stretch in the adductor.

8287
12:28:59,451 --> 12:29:12,790
Now if you don't really try to square your chest towards the floor, now from there, if you don't feel a big stretch already, we're going to come down to the elbows and we're just going to hang out in this position for 15 seconds.

8288
12:29:12,770 --> 12:29:15,997
Alright, so I should feel that stretch all through the adductors.

8289
12:29:16,037 --> 12:29:17,360
That's the inside of the leg.

8290
12:29:17,841 --> 12:29:21,209
After 15 seconds, I'm gonna switch over to that other side.

8291
12:29:21,810 --> 12:29:22,512
Same thing.

8292
12:29:22,833 --> 12:29:25,739
For my glute stretch, I'm gonna pull this knee back.

8293
12:29:26,020 --> 12:29:29,327
Now make sure that this other leg stays down on the floor.

8294
12:29:29,307 --> 12:29:33,272
So I need my right knee and my right heel down through the ground.

8295
12:29:33,592 --> 12:29:41,242
I'm not going to lift off and I'm just going to lay my head back and just pull this knee as far towards the chest as I can.

8296
12:29:41,603 --> 12:29:47,090
Now, a lot of you, as you pull, the tendency is to rise here because we're tight in the hip flexors.

8297
12:29:47,510 --> 12:29:52,817
If that's the case, try to have somebody hold that leg down or strap it down with a band.

8298
12:29:52,797 --> 12:29:57,546
or just realize that you don't have that range of motion yet and you can only pull to right here.

8299
12:29:57,947 --> 12:30:05,601
But this is a glute stretch for this left leg, but at the same time, for a lot of you, this will be a hip flexor stretch on that right side.

8300
12:30:06,102 --> 12:30:08,907
So I'm just gonna pull back, hold for 15 seconds.

8301
12:30:09,348 --> 12:30:15,379
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side, trying to get full range of motion with that other leg.

8302
12:30:15,359 --> 12:30:18,444
So for our hip flexor stretch, make sure that we have something soft.

8303
12:30:18,484 --> 12:30:20,667
I don't want you guys putting your knee on a hardwood.

8304
12:30:20,887 --> 12:30:24,553
Make sure you have a pad or a soft floor to do this stretch on.

8305
12:30:25,013 --> 12:30:26,596
So I'm just going to go on one knee.

8306
12:30:26,996 --> 12:30:29,540
This knee is going to go 90 degrees.

8307
12:30:29,600 --> 12:30:30,502
So I'm 90 there.

8308
12:30:30,862 --> 12:30:33,666
My ankle is directly underneath that knee.

8309
12:30:34,027 --> 12:30:36,310
Now from here, I just got to get up nice and tall.

8310
12:30:36,671 --> 12:30:41,358
Some of you are already going to feel a stretch in the hip flexor because we're extra tight.

8311
12:30:41,718 --> 12:30:44,843
Now from there, I'm just going to lean forward.

8312
12:30:44,823 --> 12:30:47,729
So I should have this knee back behind the hip.

8313
12:30:48,110 --> 12:30:52,880
And now from there, I'm just raising straight up and I'm going to hold.

8314
12:30:52,920 --> 12:30:54,344
All right.

8315
12:30:54,364 --> 12:31:00,256
So I feel a big stretch from here all the way down through the bottom of that hip flexor.

8316
12:31:00,998 --> 12:31:04,806
After 15 seconds, I'm going to switch over to that other side and do the same thing.

8317
12:31:04,786 --> 12:31:10,116
For my calf stretch, I'm basically going to do what they would call a downward dog in yoga.

8318
12:31:10,497 --> 12:31:13,723
So I'm here, I'm going to push back like this.

8319
12:31:13,823 --> 12:31:17,009
I'm going to straighten out my arms and now I'm just going to cross over.

8320
12:31:17,390 --> 12:31:27,148
So I'm going to put my left foot over that right foot and I'm going to try to get that right heel down towards the floor or as close as I can.

8321
12:31:27,128 --> 12:31:34,164
So I do have a straight knee, so we're stretching out what's called the gastrocnemius, that's the outside calf muscle.

8322
12:31:34,185 --> 12:31:40,339
So I shouldn't feel the stretch as much down in the ankle and the Achilles as we would on some of our hip mobility drills.

8323
12:31:40,740 --> 12:31:45,070
We should feel it on the outside of that calf muscle.

8324
12:31:45,877 --> 12:31:49,005
A lot of us aren't going to be able to get our heel all the way to the floor.

8325
12:31:49,046 --> 12:31:49,767
That's okay.

8326
12:31:50,128 --> 12:31:53,317
Just work on getting it down as low as we can.

8327
12:31:53,377 --> 12:31:57,388
After 15 seconds, I'm switching over to that other side.

8328
12:32:00,203 --> 12:32:05,289
What's up guys, quick minimum dose advice for day two and day five of phase 10.

8329
12:32:05,369 --> 12:32:07,392
So this is our plyo and agility day.

8330
12:32:07,812 --> 12:32:17,083
Now keep in mind, since we are back to back, right, yesterday we had our lower body strength workout, if you have knee problems, you're gonna wanna space this by a day.

8331
12:32:17,424 --> 12:32:25,113
You're probably gonna wanna go down to three days a week, so you would do your strength Monday, do this workout Wednesday, and then do your strength again Friday.

8332
12:32:25,273 --> 12:32:26,835
And then the next week you could flip it.

8333
12:32:26,815 --> 12:32:31,021
So you can do this workout Monday and Friday and the strength workout Wednesday.

8334
12:32:32,183 --> 12:32:35,768
For most of us, we're good to go and we're highly trained by this point.

8335
12:32:35,808 --> 12:32:40,335
We're used to this higher frequency, the more days per week, and so we should be fine.

8336
12:32:40,415 --> 12:32:47,185
But if you're waking up and your joints are sore, your knees are sore, then you might want to push this back a day.

8337
12:32:47,305 --> 12:32:50,410
Do not plow through the program.

8338
12:32:50,390 --> 12:32:52,994
Music freestyle agility, do not skip this.

8339
12:32:53,094 --> 12:32:54,776
I really want you guys to hit that.

8340
12:32:55,197 --> 12:33:00,745
If you're gonna skip anything, it would be the lateral med ball toss and the med ball acceleration change of direction.

8341
12:33:01,226 --> 12:33:10,819
Great exercises, but I understand it takes time and it takes a wall and a med ball, so some of you guys aren't gonna have the resources, so you can skip those if you have to.

8342
12:33:11,340 --> 12:33:12,702
High box depth jump.

8343
12:33:12,682 --> 12:33:14,986
This is the potent stimulus for the day.

8344
12:33:15,446 --> 12:33:22,558
This is gonna lead to probably our best vertical jump gains, but also with that comes higher stress.

8345
12:33:22,918 --> 12:33:26,104
So a high box depth jump is higher stress to the knees.

8346
12:33:26,144 --> 12:33:33,255
So if you're prone to knee injuries or patellar tendonitis, this is one that we're gonna have to scale back and listen to your body.

8347
12:33:33,235 --> 12:33:35,218
Rear foot elevated stiffness jump.

8348
12:33:35,578 --> 12:33:38,242
If you have the time and the energy, let's definitely do this.

8349
12:33:38,642 --> 12:33:41,306
Great exercise for building that elasticity.

8350
12:33:42,127 --> 12:33:44,230
If you don't have the time, it's okay to skip that.

8351
12:33:44,250 --> 12:33:46,914
20 minute dunk session, have to do it.

8352
12:33:47,354 --> 12:33:53,202
So the mandatory ones for this day is the music freestyle agility.

8353
12:33:53,182 --> 12:33:58,186
the high box depth jump as long as your knees are healthy, and the 20-minute dunk session.

8354
12:33:58,526 --> 12:34:01,189
If you get those three things, we're getting everything that we need.

8355
12:34:01,689 --> 12:34:06,493
These other ones are just extra credit that I advise you get if you do have the time.

8356
12:34:06,873 --> 12:34:08,415
So we're gonna get through a foam roller circuit.

8357
12:34:08,535 --> 12:34:10,256
I have a long roller from Skills.

8358
12:34:10,496 --> 12:34:17,382
You can do long or you can do one of the shorter rollers that you can get from Skills online, or you can just get it from Walmart or Target.

8359
12:34:17,723 --> 12:34:23,087
So the first muscle that we're gonna focus on is the glutes, one of the most important muscles for us to foam roll.

8360
12:34:23,067 --> 12:34:26,231
So you can either go this way or you can go vertical.

8361
12:34:26,571 --> 12:34:35,402
So if you go this way, we're just going to lean over to one side and then I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way up to the top of my glute.

8362
12:34:35,723 --> 12:34:42,150
Now, always, if we want more pressure, we could cross over here and add a little more pressure to make it a little tougher.

8363
12:34:42,871 --> 12:34:49,159
Or we could go vertical here and then we can lean over and get the glute horizontally as well.

8364
12:34:49,139 --> 12:34:51,543
So the next one we're going to move down the posterior chain.

8365
12:34:51,563 --> 12:34:52,986
We're going to focus on the hamstrings.

8366
12:34:53,326 --> 12:35:01,700
So I'm just going to sit on the roller and I'm going to cross over this foot and I'm just going to roll from the bottom of my glute all the way to the top of my knee.

8367
12:35:02,101 --> 12:35:13,881
So I'm just going to start in the center and then I'm gradually going to rock over a little bit so I can get the outside of my hamstrings and then I'm going to rock to the inside so I can get the inside of my hamstrings.

8368
12:35:14,114 --> 12:35:15,876
Now the next one we're going to do is the calves.

8369
12:35:16,176 --> 12:35:19,600
So we're just going to cross over and I'm going to push myself up.

8370
12:35:19,680 --> 12:35:27,889
So my hips are off the ground and I'm just going to roll basically from the bottom of the knee all the way up to the top of the ankle.

8371
12:35:27,969 --> 12:35:31,713
So we're going over that calf and also over that Achilles.

8372
12:35:32,134 --> 12:35:33,495
And our next one we're flipping over.

8373
12:35:33,555 --> 12:35:36,699
We're going hip flexor all the way down to the quad.

8374
12:35:36,759 --> 12:35:44,087
So I'm going to go on the side of the roller now and I'm just going to come down into a plank position and I'm going to roll

8375
12:35:44,067 --> 12:35:50,355
from that hip flexor all the way down towards the top of my thigh.

8376
12:35:51,076 --> 12:35:54,060
So I'm getting my whole quad in this one.

8377
12:35:54,501 --> 12:35:59,748
And if you can't get down to the quad muscles around your knee, then start here and finish.

8378
12:36:00,208 --> 12:36:06,717
And then you would scoot up and then just focus on those lower quad muscles down around the knee.

8379
12:36:06,697 --> 12:36:08,501
Our next one is going to be for the adductors.

8380
12:36:08,541 --> 12:36:09,644
That's the inside of the leg.

8381
12:36:09,824 --> 12:36:11,588
So a lot of different ways that we can do this.

8382
12:36:11,889 --> 12:36:25,701
The way that I like to go is I like to just turn that roller here, come in a plank position, bring this foot up, and I'm just rolling from the inside of the top of the knee all the way up through the groin area.

8383
12:36:27,419 --> 12:36:34,248
So we could go bent leg there or we could go straight leg there for a little bit deeper of a roll.

8384
12:36:34,429 --> 12:36:38,014
So after we're done with the foam rolling circuit, I like to get out a lacrosse ball.

8385
12:36:38,294 --> 12:36:41,558
You could use a golf ball, a baseball, anything hard.

8386
12:36:42,119 --> 12:36:44,803
And I'm just gonna really dig into that heel.

8387
12:36:44,823 --> 12:36:46,505
So I'm gonna apply some pressure in there.

8388
12:36:46,806 --> 12:36:50,811
I'm gonna go from the heel all the way down through the ball of the foot.

8389
12:36:50,791 --> 12:36:53,757
And I'm going to really loosen up that fascia.

8390
12:36:54,319 --> 12:37:00,211
A lot of times our fascia of the feet take a lot of excess stress because we're on our feet all day.

8391
12:37:00,512 --> 12:37:02,075
We're moving, we're cutting, we're jumping.

8392
12:37:02,416 --> 12:37:05,222
We got to really start to loosen that up.

8393
12:37:06,603 --> 12:37:11,256
